**Read:** Revisit the complete source readings and the evidence in the contact, wetting and surface-population investigations.

Explain how the state of a surface changes attachment and release, and why a force model, an interfacial energy, a contact-angle observation and a cleanliness measurement cannot substitute for one another. Connect microscopic interactions to the actual removal geometry and to the final destination of unwanted material.

**Work:** Defend all three investigations together. Reproduce one central calculation independently, revise a material or geometric assumption after criticism and describe the changed evidence requirements. Distinguish a checked mathematical model from a demonstrated cleaning process. Return to any shared foundation whose quantities or assumptions remain unclear.

Continue into molecular residues, contamination chemistry, cleaning, rinsing, drying and renewed exposure, then facilities, qualification and specialist production. The present surface models supply part of that formation; they do not yet define a complete cleaning process or accepted operating system.

**Return:** [Clean manufacturing and contamination control — from familiar objects to controlled processes](../Clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control.lingua#r_7D8SQ8BVQDZQBDBGC6DP0JAMZN).

